99 + 158.33\n\n274.30 ‡ 167.20\n\n0.78 0.01\n\nP‡\n\n0.019\n\n0.28 Data are presented as mean + SD. †P values for ANCOVA after adjustment for age, duration of the disease and baseline concentration of parameter; ‡P values for paired t-test. FSG fasting serum glucose, TC total cholesterol, TG triglycerides, HDL high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, AIP atherogenic index of plasma, TSH thyroid-stimulating hormone, T3 triiodothyronine T4 thyroxine, TPO thyroid peroxidase The bold data present the statistically significant values\n\nIn fact, other health promising effects of Nigella sativa like its hypolipidemic or hypoglycemic effects observed in our study and also previous reports [31, 35] could be explained by these mechanisms; we observed a strong reduction in serum LDL and TG and AIP and increase in serum HDL concentrations (P < 0.001 and P < 0.05 respectively). A mild non-significant reduction in serum FSG was also observed in Nigella sativa treated group. Serum insulin was increased in both groups and comparison of mean difference in serum insulin between groups showed no statistically significant difference.\n\n400-\n\n350-\n\nR=-0.31,P=0.04\n\n300-\n\n250-\n\n200-\n\n150-\n\n100\n\nIZ，\n\n80\n\nNes fatin-1 (ng/ml)\n\nFig. 2 Relationship between serum Nesfatin-1 and triglyceride concentrations in total participants", "width": 1654, "height": 2197, "blocks": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Farhangi et al. Lipids in Health and Disease (2018) 17:59", "bbox": {"x": 146.9, "y": 83.0, "width": 533.2, "height": 35.2}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Table 3 Metabolic parameters and thyroid hormones in treatment groups before and after intervention N", "bbox": {"x": 153.3, "y": 235.9, "width": 577.9000000000001, "height": 99.39999999999995}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Powdered Black Cumin Treated", "bbox": {"x": 258.6, "y": 309.8, "width": 293.80000000000007, "height": 32.10000000000002}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Placebo treated P", "bbox": {"x": 555.6, "y": 306.6, "width": 213